/*! \mainpage AnCH Framework
 *
 * \tableofcontents
 *
 * \section intro_sec Introduction
 *
 * AnCH Framework ("Another C++ Hack") is a set of small and independent C++ libraries for common programming
 * needs: command line, configuration, logging, events, JSON, SQL, REST, cryptography, UUID, network, dates,
 * unit tests, ... They are designed to be used as simply as possible.\n
 * \n
 * The framework is also a playground for new C++ standards: it targets C++23. Only POSIX systems are supported.\n
 * \n
 * Libraries are licensed under the LGPL v3: they can be used with the least possible restrictions.
 *
 * \section libs_sec Libraries
 *
 * | Library | Description |
 * |---------|-------------|
 * | \ref page_utils "anch-utils" | Header only utilities: thread pool, resource pool, singleton, conversions, ... |
 * | \ref page_cli "anch-cli" | Command line arguments parsing and terminal formatting |
 * | \ref page_events "anch-events" | Observers, event bus and event handlers |
 * | \ref page_cutils "anch-cutils" | C++ streams over C style read and write functions |
 * | \ref page_network "anch-network" | TCP (as C++ streams) and UDP sockets, IPv4 and IPv6 |
 * | \ref page_ut "anch-ut" | Unit tests library, integrated with CTest |
 * | \ref page_uuid "anch-uuid" | UUID generation and parsing (RFC 9562) |
 * | \ref page_date "anch-date" | Dates, formatting and parsing, RFC 3339 |
 * | \ref page_ini "anch-ini" | INI files parser |
 * | \ref page_res "anch-conf" | Configuration cascade (includes, profiles) and placeholders resolution |
 * | \ref page_logger "anch-log" | Configurable loggers and writers, stream API with compile-time levels |
 * | \ref page_device "anch-device" | Network interfaces and CPU features |
 * | \ref page_val "anch-val" | Fields constraints and validation, shared by formats (JSON, ...) |
 * | \ref page_json "anch-json" | JSON serialization and deserialization |
 * | \ref page_crypto "anch-crypto" | Hashes, HMAC, Base64, AES and block cipher modes (not audited) |
 * | \ref page_sql "anch-sql" | SQL abstraction layer and connection pools for MySQL/MariaDB, PostgreSQL and SQLite |
 * | \ref page_csv "anch-csv" | CSV records reading and writing (RFC 4180), as streams |
 * | \ref page_codec "anch-codec" | Codecs (media types, negotiation) and format plugins: JSON, Protocol Buffers, Thrift |
 * | \ref page_rest "anch-rest" | REST API declaration (nginx module), HTTP client (libcurl), Protobuf / gRPC / Connect and Thrift RPC |
 * | \ref page_oas "anch-oas" | OpenAPI 3.0 models generator (\c anch-oas-gen ) |
 *
 * \c anch-app (application bootstrap) is a work in progress and is not documented yet.
 *
 * \section prereq_sec Prerequisites
 *
 * - GCC 13 or later (C++23)
 * - CMake 3.25 or later
 * - POSIX system
 *
 * Optional dependencies:
 * - SQL drivers: libmariadb (or libmysqlclient), libpq, libsqlite3
 * - anch-rest curl transport: libcurl
 * - anch-rest nginx module: nginx sources (built with \c --with-threads )
 * - anch-rest Protobuf codecs and RPC: Protobuf (and gRPC for the gRPC server), built when they are found
 * - anch-rest Thrift codecs and RPC: Thrift and Boost headers, built when they are found
 *
 * \section install_sec Installation
 *
 * Build, test and install with CMake presets (\c release , \c debug , \c asan , \c no-db ):
 * \code{.sh}
cmake --preset release
cmake --build --preset release
ctest --preset release
cmake --install build/release --prefix /usr/local
\endcode
 *
 * Main options: \c ANCH_BUILD_TESTS , \c BUILD_SHARED_LIBS , optional libraries (\c ANCH_BUILD_CRYPTO ,
 * \c ANCH_BUILD_SQL , \c ANCH_BUILD_OAS , \c ANCH_BUILD_REST ) and features (\c ANCH_UUID , \c ANCH_DATE ,
 * \c ANCH_SQL_MYSQL , \c ANCH_SQL_POSTGRESQL , \c ANCH_SQL_SQLITE3 , \c ANCH_REST_CURL , \c ANCH_REST_NGINX ,
 * \c ANCH_LOG_MIN_LEVEL , ...).\n
 * \n
 * Installed libraries are used with their CMake targets:
 * \code{.cmake}
find_package(Anch REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(my-app PRIVATE anch::conf anch::log anch::json)
\endcode
 *
 * \section deps_sec Libraries dependencies
 *
 * - anch-utils, anch-cli, anch-cutils, anch-date, anch-ini, anch-uuid: none
 * - anch-events: anch-utils
 * - anch-network: anch-cutils
 * - anch-ut: anch-cli
 * - anch-conf: anch-ini, anch-utils
 * - anch-log: anch-conf, anch-date, anch-utils
 * - anch-device: anch-uuid, anch-utils
 * - anch-val: none ; anch-uuid and anch-date with \c ANCH_UUID and \c ANCH_DATE options
 * - anch-json: anch-events, anch-utils, anch-val ; anch-uuid and anch-date with \c ANCH_UUID and \c ANCH_DATE options
 * - anch-csv: anch-utils
 * - anch-crypto: anch-uuid, anch-utils
 * - anch-sql: anch-conf, anch-date, anch-utils
 * - anch-codec: anch-utils ; plugins: anch-codec-json (anch-json), anch-codec-protobuf (Protobuf), anch-codec-thrift
 *   (Thrift, Boost headers)
 * - anch-rest: anch-codec-json, anch-uuid
 * - anch-oas: anch-json
 *
 * \section version_sec Versions
 *
 * Current version is \b 0.2 . Versions are numbered A.B.C: until 1.0.0, backward compatibility is not ensured
 * and API can change on every B change. From 1.0.0, A changes will break API, B changes will add features and
 * C changes will fix bugs. Shared libraries SONAME holds A and B (\c libanch-conf.so.0.2 ).\n
 * \n
 * Until 1.0.0, APIs are changed or removed directly, without deprecation: breaking changes are listed in the
 * \ref changelog "changelog". From 1.0.0, B and C versions keep backward compatibility: deprecated APIs are marked
 * with \c [[deprecated]] (compilation warning) and \c \\deprecated in their documentation, with their replacement,
 * and are removed in the next A version.\n
 * \n
 * Shared libraries are built with hidden symbols visibility: only the public API is exported, with the
 * \c ANCH_\<NAME\>_EXPORT macros of the generated \c anch-\<name\>-export.hpp headers.\n
 * \n
 * Version 0.2 brings: single CMake project with presets and package, C++23 and GCC 13, rewritten anch-network,
 * anch-date, anch-rest and anch-conf cascade, anch-log stream API, anch-oas generator, RFC 3339 dates in
 * anch-json, many fixes found with sanitizers and fuzzing, and a CI (GCC, Clang, sanitizers, coverage, fuzzing,
 * installation). Changes and breaking changes of each version are listed in the \ref changelog "changelog".
 *
 * \section ai_sec AI assisted development
 *
 * Since version 0.2, part of the work on AnCH Framework has been done with an AI assistant
 * (<a href="https://claude.com/claude-code">Claude Code</a>, by Anthropic): code analyses, bug fixes (several
 * found with the sanitizers), rewrites (anch-rest, anch-network, anch-date, ...), tests, CI and this
 * documentation. Design decisions remain the maintainer's.\n
 * \n
 * Commits written with the assistant are identified in the git history with a \c Co-Authored-By: \c Claude
 * trailer.
 */
